::GEO_U04_L07_Q01::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L07_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.5<br><br>Question:<br>A right triangle has legs 9 and 12. What is the hypotenuse? {
=A. 15#Use the Pythagorean Theorem: 9<sup>2</sup> + 12<sup>2</sup> = 225, so c = 15.
~B. 21#Adding the legs gives 21, but side lengths in a right triangle use a sum of squares.
~C. 3#Subtracting the legs does not find the hypotenuse.
~D. &radic;21#The legs must be squared before adding.
}

::GEO_U04_L07_Q02::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L07_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.5<br><br>Question:<br>A right triangle has hypotenuse 10 and one leg 6. What is the other leg? {
~A. 4#Subtracting 6 from 10 ignores the squared side relationship.
=B. 8#Use b<sup>2</sup> = 10<sup>2</sup> - 6<sup>2</sup> = 64, so b = 8.
~C. 16#Adding 10 and 6 does not find a missing leg.
~D. &radic;136#Adding the squares would find a larger hypotenuse, but the hypotenuse is already known.
}

::GEO_U04_L07_Q03::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L07_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.5<br><br>Question:<br>A ladder is 20 ft long and makes a right triangle with a wall. The base is 12 ft from the wall. How high does the ladder reach? {
~A. 8 ft#Subtracting distances does not use the Pythagorean Theorem.
~B. 32 ft#This adds the given lengths instead of using squares.
=C. 16 ft#The ladder is the hypotenuse: h<sup>2</sup> + 12<sup>2</sup> = 20<sup>2</sup>, so h = 16 ft.
~D. &radic;544 ft#Adding 20<sup>2</sup> and 12<sup>2</sup> treats both given sides as legs, but 20 is the hypotenuse.
}

::GEO_U04_L07_Q04::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L07_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.5<br><br>Question:<br>In a right triangle, sin A = <sup>5</sup>&frasl;<sub>13</sub>. If the hypotenuse is 26, what is the side opposite angle A? {
~A. 5#5 is the ratio numerator, not the scaled side length when the hypotenuse is 26.
~B. 13#13 is part of the ratio, not the opposite side in the larger triangle.
~C. 26#The hypotenuse is 26; the opposite side must match the sine ratio.
=D. 10#Set <sup>opposite</sup>&frasl;<sub>26</sub> = <sup>5</sup>&frasl;<sub>13</sub>. Since 26 is twice 13, the opposite side is 10.
}

::GEO_U04_L07_Q05::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L07_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.5<br><br>Question:<br>A right triangle has angle A with adjacent side 12 and hypotenuse 13. Which ratio can be used to find angle A? {
=A. cos A = <sup>12</sup>&frasl;<sub>13</sub>#Cosine uses adjacent over hypotenuse, so cos A = <sup>12</sup>&frasl;<sub>13</sub>.
~B. sin A = <sup>12</sup>&frasl;<sub>13</sub>#Sine uses opposite over hypotenuse, not adjacent over hypotenuse.
~C. tan A = <sup>12</sup>&frasl;<sub>13</sub>#Tangent uses opposite over adjacent and does not use the hypotenuse.
~D. cos A = <sup>13</sup>&frasl;<sub>12</sub>#This reverses adjacent over hypotenuse.
}

::GEO_U04_L07_Q06::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L07_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.5<br><br>Question:<br>A ramp rises 3 ft over a horizontal distance of 4 ft. What is the ramp length? {
~A. 7 ft#Adding the legs does not find the hypotenuse.
=B. 5 ft#The rise and run are legs: 3<sup>2</sup> + 4<sup>2</sup> = 25, so the ramp length is 5 ft.
~C. 1 ft#Subtracting the legs does not describe the ramp length.
~D. 12 ft#Multiplying rise and run does not use the Pythagorean Theorem.
}

::GEO_U04_L07_Q07::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L07_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.5<br><br>Question:<br>Which setup finds a building height h when a 30&deg; angle of elevation has opposite side h and adjacent side 40? {
~A. sin 30&deg; = <sup>40</sup>&frasl;<sub>h</sub>#Sine uses opposite over hypotenuse, and 40 is adjacent here.
~B. cos 30&deg; = <sup>h</sup>&frasl;<sub>40</sub>#Cosine uses adjacent over hypotenuse, not opposite over adjacent.
=C. tan 30&deg; = <sup>h</sup>&frasl;<sub>40</sub>#Tangent uses opposite over adjacent, so h belongs over 40.
~D. tan 30&deg; = <sup>40</sup>&frasl;<sub>h</sub>#This reverses opposite and adjacent.
}

::GEO_U04_L07_Q08::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L07_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.5<br><br>Question:<br>A right triangle has opposite side 7 and adjacent side 24 relative to angle B. Which ratio helps find angle B? {
~A. sin B = <sup>7</sup>&frasl;<sub>24</sub>#Sine requires the hypotenuse, not adjacent side.
~B. cos B = <sup>7</sup>&frasl;<sub>24</sub>#Cosine uses adjacent over hypotenuse.
~C. tan B = <sup>24</sup>&frasl;<sub>7</sub>#This reverses the tangent ratio.
=D. tan B = <sup>7</sup>&frasl;<sub>24</sub>#Tangent uses opposite over adjacent, so tan B = <sup>7</sup>&frasl;<sub>24</sub>.
}

::GEO_U04_L07_Q09::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L07_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.5<br><br>Question:<br>A diagonal path crosses a rectangular field that is 30 m by 40 m. How long is the diagonal? {
=A. 50 m#The diagonal is the hypotenuse: 30<sup>2</sup> + 40<sup>2</sup> = 2500, so the diagonal is 50 m.
~B. 70 m#Adding side lengths gives a path around two sides, not the diagonal.
~C. 10 m#Subtracting side lengths does not find a diagonal.
~D. 1200 m#Multiplying side lengths gives area, not diagonal length.
}

::GEO_U04_L07_Q10::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L07_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.5<br><br>Question:<br>Why is labeling a right-triangle diagram important before solving? {
~A. It changes the side lengths.#Labeling does not change measurements; it clarifies how to use them.
=B. It identifies which sides match the equation or trig ratio.#Accurate labels prevent mixing up legs, hypotenuse, opposite, and adjacent sides.
~C. It removes the need for calculations.#Labels support calculations but do not replace them.
~D. It makes every triangle similar.#Labels do not prove similarity; they organize known information.
}

::GEO_U04_L07_Q11::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L07_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.5<br><br>Question:<br>A right triangle has legs 9 and 12. What is the hypotenuse? {
~A. 21#Adding the legs gives 21, but side lengths in a right triangle use a sum of squares.
~B. 3#Subtracting the legs does not find the hypotenuse.
=C. 15#Use the Pythagorean Theorem: 9<sup>2</sup> + 12<sup>2</sup> = 225, so c = 15.
~D. &radic;21#The legs must be squared before adding.
}

::GEO_U04_L07_Q12::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L07_Q12<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.5<br><br>Question:<br>A right triangle has hypotenuse 10 and one leg 6. What is the other leg? {
~A. 4#Subtracting 6 from 10 ignores the squared side relationship.
~B. 16#Adding 10 and 6 does not find a missing leg.
~C. &radic;136#Adding the squares would find a larger hypotenuse, but the hypotenuse is already known.
=D. 8#Use b<sup>2</sup> = 10<sup>2</sup> - 6<sup>2</sup> = 64, so b = 8.
}

::GEO_U04_L07_Q13::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L07_Q13<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.5<br><br>Question:<br>A ladder is 20 ft long and makes a right triangle with a wall. The base is 12 ft from the wall. How high does the ladder reach? {
=A. 16 ft#The ladder is the hypotenuse: h<sup>2</sup> + 12<sup>2</sup> = 20<sup>2</sup>, so h = 16 ft.
~B. 8 ft#Subtracting distances does not use the Pythagorean Theorem.
~C. 32 ft#This adds the given lengths instead of using squares.
~D. &radic;544 ft#Adding 20<sup>2</sup> and 12<sup>2</sup> treats both given sides as legs, but 20 is the hypotenuse.
}

::GEO_U04_L07_Q14::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L07_Q14<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.5<br><br>Question:<br>In a right triangle, sin A = <sup>5</sup>&frasl;<sub>13</sub>. If the hypotenuse is 26, what is the side opposite angle A? {
~A. 5#5 is the ratio numerator, not the scaled side length when the hypotenuse is 26.
=B. 10#Set <sup>opposite</sup>&frasl;<sub>26</sub> = <sup>5</sup>&frasl;<sub>13</sub>. Since 26 is twice 13, the opposite side is 10.
~C. 13#13 is part of the ratio, not the opposite side in the larger triangle.
~D. 26#The hypotenuse is 26; the opposite side must match the sine ratio.
}

::GEO_U04_L07_Q15::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L07_Q15<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.5<br><br>Question:<br>A right triangle has angle A with adjacent side 12 and hypotenuse 13. Which ratio can be used to find angle A? {
~A. sin A = <sup>12</sup>&frasl;<sub>13</sub>#Sine uses opposite over hypotenuse, not adjacent over hypotenuse.
~B. tan A = <sup>12</sup>&frasl;<sub>13</sub>#Tangent uses opposite over adjacent and does not use the hypotenuse.
=C. cos A = <sup>12</sup>&frasl;<sub>13</sub>#Cosine uses adjacent over hypotenuse, so cos A = <sup>12</sup>&frasl;<sub>13</sub>.
~D. cos A = <sup>13</sup>&frasl;<sub>12</sub>#This reverses adjacent over hypotenuse.
}

::GEO_U04_L07_Q16::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L07_Q16<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.5<br><br>Question:<br>A ramp rises 3 ft over a horizontal distance of 4 ft. What is the ramp length? {
~A. 7 ft#Adding the legs does not find the hypotenuse.
~B. 1 ft#Subtracting the legs does not describe the ramp length.
~C. 12 ft#Multiplying rise and run does not use the Pythagorean Theorem.
=D. 5 ft#The rise and run are legs: 3<sup>2</sup> + 4<sup>2</sup> = 25, so the ramp length is 5 ft.
}

::GEO_U04_L07_Q17::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L07_Q17<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.5<br><br>Question:<br>Which setup finds a building height h when a 30&deg; angle of elevation has opposite side h and adjacent side 40? {
=A. tan 30&deg; = <sup>h</sup>&frasl;<sub>40</sub>#Tangent uses opposite over adjacent, so h belongs over 40.
~B. sin 30&deg; = <sup>40</sup>&frasl;<sub>h</sub>#Sine uses opposite over hypotenuse, and 40 is adjacent here.
~C. cos 30&deg; = <sup>h</sup>&frasl;<sub>40</sub>#Cosine uses adjacent over hypotenuse, not opposite over adjacent.
~D. tan 30&deg; = <sup>40</sup>&frasl;<sub>h</sub>#This reverses opposite and adjacent.
}

::GEO_U04_L07_Q18::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L07_Q18<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.5<br><br>Question:<br>A right triangle has opposite side 7 and adjacent side 24 relative to angle B. Which ratio helps find angle B? {
